Installing Check2
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
If you are upgrading from a Previous Arexx version, you should delete
pretty much everything related to it..

Step 1    -- Making sure PIPE is ready.
~~~~~~
Make sure PIPE: is mounted in your startup sequence. 
And also 'in' your MountList, which is located in DEVS:.
Also DO NOT USE ARP's 'MOUNT' command. 
The PIPE: handlers are included with Amiga-DOS 1.3/2.0

Step 2    -- Copying - Editing sys.validation.
~~~~~~
Copy my "sys.validation" to your "SYSTEXT:" directory.
Or create your own. But it must contain an MCI command
to run Check2. See mine as an example.

Step 3    --  Installing the checker itself.
~~~~~~
Copy Check2 to your CNET: directory. Note you can edit
the sys.validation file to change where Check2 is
searched for.

Step 4   -- What your "C:" dir should contain.
~~~~~~
All the paths for these are editable in Check2.cfg

Using any versions other than those listed
may produce invalid checking rusults. Or possibly
crashing your bbs.

RUN.........TESTED WITH AMIGA DOS'S ONLY!
Warpck......WarpCheck v1.4 by © Mike Thomas. 
HSCHK.......High Speed DMS check V4.02 © By Vince
Zoom........v5.3 © MXM - Olaf 'Olsen' Barthel
LhA.........LhA V1.11 - Copyright 1991,92 Stefan Boberg.
Append......Appends text to Text files, faster than using join,
            delete, and rename.. (Included in this archive)
Pkax........v1.0 © PKWARE To quickly test/extract ARC files.
Zoo.........AmigaZoo V2.1 beta Copyright 1991 Rahul Dhesi & J. Brian Waters
Unzip.......v4.1 of 5-13-91 (C) 1989 Samuel H. Smith
Unarj.......V0.5 - Copyright (c) 1991 Peer Hasselmeyer
Zip.........V 0.92 - AMIGA version  by Ross Martin & Steve Lewis

Most of the above were, or are on Future World, or any good bbs.
(PS if you have newer versions of any of these please upload to
Future World).

Step 5    -- What your S: directory MIGHT contain.
~~~~~~
These are the ascii/ansi texts you may need in your S: dir.


HSC.TXT......Text to be added to DMS files, 20k or smaller.
             I suggest using a text file only a few K.
             10k added to each DMS uploaded is quite a
             bit of wasted disk space. Its all in
             what your willing to sacrifice.

Now for the conversion scripts.. (Delete any old
Conv??? scripts from Arexx versions).

There are 7 conversion scripts included in this archive.. 
Grouped into 2 catagories.

Catagory 1:
The 3 scripts, ConvArc, ConvZip, and ConvZoo will 
actually convert archives to an LHA file.
(you will still have to 'AO' to get them online,
automatic adoption is next on the list, but will
be a item only in registered versions)

Catagory 2:
The other 4 scripts ConvArc2, ConvZip2, and ConvZoo2, and 
ConvLzh will ADD text to these archives. And will do no 
conversion. If you wish to use any of these scripts, you 
must rename them, and remove the "2". (if needed)

You may use either group, none (set Check2.cfg to 0), or you may
mix the two groups. Thus you could convert Zoo, and Arc, and just
add text to Zip files, which have decent compression.

Now if you have the flag set to 1 in Check2.cfg this
will tell the upload checker to execute these scripts.
Otherwise NOTHING will be done to ANY of these archives.

You may also edit these conversion scripts to change default
path to something other than RAM: if you are low on memory.

More on the scripts, there are three items passed to each
script file.. The name and location of the actual file.
The name and location if the file is to be converted to LHA.
And the filename without any extensions..
Lets say the uploaded file name is 'TEST.ZOO'.
Check2 will send a command to dos looking something like:

run execute s:convzoo udbase0:uploads/test.lha udbase0:uploads/test.zoo test



Step 6  -- Setting UD/Message bases up to run the checker.
~~~~~~
You MUST set your udbases to validate uploads...Use the EL
commmand and then enter "$" for option flags, and when it asks
'Validate Uploads' enter yes. This text will then be displayed
to ANY user that uploads. Depending on how your bbs is set up,
you may want to edit access groups so that users uploads are
auto-validated, and can be downloaded. In either case, this
program will do the job. Also this will mean the program is run
after any upload. 
If you want new uploads to NOT be seen, then do not change user
groups access to Autovalidate..But if you want new uploads to
be available for immediate download, then set all your users
to autovalidate.. The sys.validation file will be displayed
no matter what the users access is. 


Step 7   -- Adding a command to BBSMENU.
~~~~~~
For local/manual testing You must add a command to BBSMENU. 
The best place to add this is section 4, ud/base commmands. 
Add the following line to the end of the commands listed. 

	CU, Check Uploads `23 |\#2Check2\

When you type CU ,it will run Check2 from the CNET: dir.
And will check any "selected" files.


Step 8    -- Editing the Config
~~~~~~
Copy the file Check2.cfg to your "sysdata:" Directory.
Load your favorite text editor and edit each line
as needed. Following is the example config, with
explanations for each line. Future versions will
support the abiltiy to comment the config file.
SO for now do not add any lines, and be careful
editing this.. Appending spaces to lines may
screw this program up severely.

******* Example Config file:

0				;(0 or 1) 1 = execute conv??? scripts. See step 5
s:convlzh			;Path/name of Lzh/LHA script
s:append.txt			;Text File to be Appended to Text Uploads
c2:HSCHK			;Path/name of DMS checker HSCHK
c:LHA				;Path/name of LHA
c:append			;Path/name of APPEND
c:warpck			;Path/name of WarpCheck
c:unzip				;Path/name of UnZip
c:zoo				;Path/name of Zoo 
c:pkax				;Path/name of PKAX
c:unarj				;Path/name of Unarj
c:zoom				;Path/name of Zoom 5.3
\n2\c4Checking :\c7 %s\n2	
 Failed Integrity Check		;Actual DOS & BBS File Comment.
\n1\c3Failed, Commenting file.\n2
\n1\c6Passed!\n2
\n1\c5Be Patient\c6 %s \c5files take a minute.
\n2\c1%d \c4Files Uploaded\n
\c6\n2Unfinished cannot test : %s\n2
\w1				;The pause before reading pipe.
s:convarc			;Path/name of ARC script
s:convzip			;Path/name of ZIP script
s:convzoo			;Path/name of ZOO script





A quick note about the pause.. On my A3000 It did not need the
pause, but on slower machines it seems necesarry. :(
The \W1 has been tested on an Amiga 2000 with a 68000.
 
20 Lines total in the config file, this is checked for
by "Check2".

Make sure you are running the correct versions of those
programs as stated in step 4.

Step 9   -- Manual - Local testing.
~~~~~~

Manually test this program out by selecting a few archives
in an upload directory. And then using the CU command to
start the upload checker.

NOTE: Check2 will not check files on different partitions.. 
This is not a bug! If a user uploads, all of his uploads will 
go to ONE partition. CNet does not split upload sessions
between partitions.

Make sure you test every archive which you allow as uploads.


KNOWN PROBLEMS:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

None really, this is my third release! And those earlier
problems seem to be fixed.

I would not suggest this program for BIG multiline bbs's. Four 
lines or so on a 68030 with A2232, would probably be no problem. 
The main reason being those 'c' executbles are quite the CPU hog. 
Espically when testing an LHA..
I am running 2 9600 nodes with no problems on an Amiga 3000 with 
an A2232 serial card..

If you notice this program locking up, your CPU may not be 'crashed' 
type in a Shell or CLI:
 
TYPE >PIPE:ctstX             X is the port # which seems to be crashed.


******************************************************************
*  I also do not use sub-dirs with spaces in them. Commodore	 *
*  even recommends you NOT use spaces in directory names	 *
*  and filenames. In other words DONT USE SPACES!		 *
*  Cnet supports different SUB names, and DOS names so use it!	 *
******************************************************************

